      en: "Morro de Fundão is a gentle but prominent elevation in the northern, drier part of São Tomé, in the Lobata district. In contrast to the rugged, forested peaks of the south, this hill is surrounded by a savanna-like landscape and agricultural land. Geologically, it consists of older, more eroded volcanic rock. Its location offers a panoramic view over the north coast, the bay of Ana Chaves, and the capital city, São Tomé. Historically, this area was one of the first to be settled and cultivated by the Portuguese due to its proximity to the capital and flatter topography. Today, it is a mixture of small farms, villages, and bushland recovering from the rigors of the plantation economy."
